# AusMT public front door (C47 public bridge) — the VPS edge for the public demo name.
#
# TOPOLOGY (maintainer/C47-PublicBridge.md): internet -> THIS Caddy on a small Sydney VPS (public IP,
# DNS A/AAAA at the registrar) -> over the tailnet (WireGuard) -> the box's dedicated READER-ONLY
# listener (Caddyfile :8081, published loopback 127.0.0.1:8445, fronted by `tailscale serve --tcp`).
# The box gets NO inbound internet exposure and NO firewall changes. The curator/admin workbench stays
# tailnet-only exactly as before; this file routes ONLY the PUBLIC submission subset of /gateway (submit,
# request-key, healthz, status) plus the public Add Survey page, and refuses every other /gateway surface
# (an MT user who clicks Add Survey must be able to reach the page and lodge a
# survey, so the contribution flow is public while the workbench stays walled: see WALL 1 below).
#
# This Caddy is the TRUE edge: nothing sits in front of it (no Cloudflare — a rejected alternative,
# see the record). So the direct peer IS the real client, and this file deliberately sets NO
# `trusted_proxies` — it must not honour a client-supplied X-Forwarded-For (that would let a caller
# spoof the logged client_ip). The masked access log below is therefore taken on the genuine client.
#
# NO hostname is baked into git: the public name and the box upstream are `{$ENV}` placeholders the
# compose file supplies from deploy/frontdoor/.env (config-side names only, same discipline as the box
# stack). `caddy validate` in the install script fails the deploy LOUDLY on any slip.
{
	# Contact address for the ACME account (Let's Encrypt) that issues the public certificate. Config
	# only — no personal hostname. Supplied from .env.
	email {$AUSMT_ACME_EMAIL}

	# Admin API on a UNIX SOCKET, not a TCP port (ops-hardening O1). The original rationale
	# for `admin off` was "a static edge needs no extra LISTENING PORT" — a unix socket honours that
	# exactly: it opens NO network port (the socket lives in the container's own /run, not on the host,
	# even under network_mode: host), so there is no new internet- or host-reachable surface. What it
	# buys is a working `caddy reload`: install-frontdoor.sh reloads the running edge in place after a
	# Caddyfile change instead of leaving it serving the OLD wall (the 2026-07 stale-config incident).
	# `caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile` reads this admin address from the file. If you would
	# rather forgo in-place reload entirely, set `admin off` here again: the installer detects the reload
	# failure and falls back to `docker compose restart frontdoor` (a ~1s bounce; certs persist in the
	# caddy_data volume), so the deploy is still correct, just not graceful.
	admin unix//run/caddy-admin.sock

	# NOTE: NO `servers { trusted_proxies ... }` here — see the header comment. This edge trusts no
	# forwarded address; client_ip == the real remote peer, which the log block masks at write time.
}

# ---- The ONE transport to the box reader (serve-path tuning) --------------------------
# Every proxy to the box imports this snippet, so there is exactly one place the upstream transport
# can be got wrong. It dials h2c: tailscale serve forwards :8445 as a RAW TCP tunnel (RUNBOOK
# `--tcp=8445`), so the h2c bytes pass end to end to the box reader listener, which accepts h2c
# (deploy/docker/caddy/Caddyfile `servers :8081`). This un-serialises the browser's parallel
# streams; before it, 15 h2 requests funnelled into fresh HTTP/1.1 upstream connections and the
# cold-load script TTFBs stepped 1.1s / 1.6s / 2.2s (the measured staircase). keepalive keeps the
# multiplexed connections warm between page loads.
#
# DEPLOY ORDER: the BOX ships first. h2c here has no h1 fallback, so a front door updated before
# the box reader accepts h2c would 502 the whole site. Box litany, then install-frontdoor.sh.
(box_upstream) {
	reverse_proxy {$AUSMT_BOX_READER_UPSTREAM} {
		transport http {
			versions h2c 2
			keepalive 90s
			keepalive_idle_conns 64
		}
	}
}

# The public site. A bare hostname address turns on Caddy's automatic HTTPS: it obtains + renews a
# certificate for {$AUSMT_PUBLIC_NAME} over ACME and AUTOMATICALLY serves an HTTP->HTTPS redirect on
# :80 for the same name (C47 invariant d). Do NOT add `auto_https off` — that would drop both the cert
# and the redirect.
{$AUSMT_PUBLIC_NAME} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# ---- C45 masked-at-edge access log — AT THE FRONT DOOR on public traffic (C47 invariant c) -----
	# This is the analytics feed now: the public client is masked HERE (IPv4 -> /24, IPv6 -> /48) so a
	# FULL IP never touches disk, with the SAME at-edge guarantees as the box's C45 block. The log is
	# shipped over the tailnet to the box (ship-frontdoor-logs.sh) where the C45 aggregator folds it.
	# The filter masks the client-address fields AND deletes every header that can carry a full client
	# address (X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP / Forwarded — even though we trust none, a caller can still
	# SEND them and they must never land in the log) or a credential/PII (Cookie / Authorization /
	# Set-Cookie / Referer). Written to a DISTINCT filename (access-frontdoor.json) so that, once
	# shipped into the box log dir beside the box's own access.json, the aggregator's `access*.json`
	# glob folds it without collision. VPS-side retention: rotated + kept 7 days (roll_keep_for 168h),
	# a short debugging tail; the shipped-and-folded copy on the box is the durable record.
	#
	# ROLLED FILES STAY PLAIN JSON (roll_uncompressed). Caddy gzips a rolled log by default, and both
	# the ship filter and the box's fold glob key on the plain `.json` name, so a compressed roll would
	# never be shipped and never be folded. Every line that rolled before the daily 03:25 pull would be
	# deleted by this listener's own 7-day retention having reached the box's aggregate nowhere.
	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/access-frontdoor.json {
			roll_size 10mb
			roll_keep 5
			roll_keep_for 168h
			roll_uncompressed
		}
		format filter {
			wrap json
			fields {
				request>remote_ip ip_mask {
					ipv4 24
					ipv6 48
				}
				request>client_ip ip_mask {
					ipv4 24
					ipv6 48
				}
				# Redact the /gateway/status/<token> capability from the logged URI - this is the
				# PUBLIC edge where a status hit actually lands. The token is a secrets.token_urlsafe(32)
				# bearer whose DB form is SHA-256 hashed; un-redacted it lands in this masked log verbatim
				# and ships to the box. The regexp field op rewrites ONLY the token segment (the
				# /gateway/status/ prefix, any query and every other URI are left intact). Caddy log
				# `filter` supports regexp on request>uri (confirmed v2.11.4; image caddy:2-alpine).
				request>uri regexp "^(/gateway/status/)[^/?]+" "${1}REDACTED"
				request>headers>Cookie delete
				request>headers>Authorization delete
				request>headers>Set-Cookie delete
				request>headers>X-Forwarded-For delete
				request>headers>X-Real-IP delete
				request>headers>Forwarded delete
				request>headers>Referer delete
			}
		}
	}

	# Baseline security headers + HSTS (public TLS is now in force, so tell browsers to stay on HTTPS).
	# CSP is NOT set here — it rides through from the box reader listener's per-page policy via the
	# reverse_proxy below, so the three pages keep their exact C45/portal CSPs.
	header {
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		Referrer-Policy no-referrer
		X-Frame-Options DENY
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
	}

	# ---- WALL 1: explicit PUBLIC-SUBSET allowlist, deny-by-default for the gateway surface -----------
	# Wall 1 is not a blanket "refuse the whole gateway" deny. The Add Survey contribution flow is
	# PUBLIC, so wall 1 is an ALLOWLIST of the exact FIVE public entry
	# points, with a deny-by-default 404 for everything else under /gateway. The allow `handle`s run
	# BEFORE the deny `handle`, and `handle` blocks are mutually exclusive and evaluated in SOURCE ORDER,
	# so a public route is proxied and every other /gateway path falls through to the explicit 404. Every
	# matcher is METHOD-SCOPED: each gateway route matches only the verb its FastAPI route serves, and
	# add-survey.html is GET-only — a wrong-method hit (e.g. GET /gateway/submit) matches no allow and is
	# refused like any other non-public path.
	#
	# THE PUBLIC SUBSET (read gateway/app.py — these are the ONLY routes reachable at the public name):
	#   * GET  /add-survey.html (+ trailing slash) — the contribution page (map, ROR autocomplete, the
	#                        Submit-to-AusMT button). Static; served by the box reader.
	#   * POST /gateway/submit          — the direct-upload endpoint the Submit button POSTs to.
	#   * POST /gateway/request-key - self-serve email key issuance.
	#   * GET  /gateway/healthz         — the liveness probe the page uses to reveal the Submit button.
	#   * GET  /gateway/status/*        — the capability-token submission-status page (design §3).
	# Everything else under /gateway — the ENTIRE curator/admin workbench (login, queue, publish, serve
	# control, passkey enrolment, analytics, uploader-key management) — is an EXPLICIT 404. The bare
	# `/gateway` is enumerated alongside `/gateway/*` because `/gateway/*` does not match a path with no
	# trailing slash, so wall 1 stays SELF-COMPLETE in both slash forms; a non-GET /add-survey.html is
	# refused there too (only the GET is carved out above).
	#
	# Wall 2 (the box reader listener, deploy/docker/caddy/Caddyfile :8081) is an INDEPENDENT allowlist
	# of the SAME subset behind the port-scoped tailnet ACL: it proxies only these four gateway routes to
	# the gateway container and serves add-survey.html itself, refusing every other /gateway path on its
	# own (C47 invariant b). Two independent allowlists of one subset — a breach needs BOTH to widen.
	@public_add_survey {
		method GET
		path /add-survey.html /add-survey.html/
	}
	@public_gw_submit {
		method POST
		path /gateway/submit
	}
	@public_gw_request_key {
		method POST
		path /gateway/request-key
	}
	@public_gw_healthz {
		method GET
		path /gateway/healthz
	}
	@public_gw_status {
		method GET
		path /gateway/status/*
	}
	handle @public_add_survey {
		import box_upstream
	}
	handle @public_gw_submit {
		import box_upstream
	}
	handle @public_gw_request_key {
		import box_upstream
	}
	handle @public_gw_healthz {
		import box_upstream
	}
	handle @public_gw_status {
		import box_upstream
	}

	# ---- PATH-URL CONTRACT (tier 3: prerendered entity landing pages) -------------------------------
	# /surveys/<slug>, /stations/<ausmt_id> and /collections/<id> are the PUBLISHED URL contract for
	# the portal's three entity kinds: the path SHAPE is what gets published (emails, talks, papers,
	# programme pages, DOI metadata), and the machinery behind it can upgrade without breaking a
	# link. Tier 1 permanently redirected these shapes into the SPA's hash routes; TIER 3 serves
	# REAL static landing pages at the same shapes (engine pages/ products, box @entityPage), so the
	# entity forms now PASS THROUGH to the reader like any portal path: a crawler receives indexable
	# HTML with a canonical at this exact URL, and a human receives the landing page with its
	# interactive-portal deep link. No published URL changed, which is precisely what the contract
	# promised when the tiers were designed. The bare prefixes now split: /surveys and /collections
	# serve prerendered INDEX pages from the same pages/ product and pass through like any entity
	# shape, while a bare /stations (and its trailing-slash twin) still 301s to the portal root,
	# query preserved via the lazily-evaluated {ausmt_qs} map below (map is ordered ahead of every
	# handle; its source is read at redirect time).
	# {ausmt_pathurl_rest}: the raw request URI up to (not including) any '?', evaluated lazily.
	# Consumed by the TIME-SERIES HAND-OFF table below as its map source; declared here beside its
	# query twin so the two derived-URI views live in one place.
	map {http.request.uri} {ausmt_pathurl_rest} {
		~^([^?]*) "${1}"
		default ""
	}
	map {http.request.uri} {ausmt_qs} {
		~\?(.*)$ "?${1}"
		default ""
	}
	# A bare /stations has nothing to list and never will: the 2,625 station pages are served for
	# the URL contract but are deliberately noindex and unadvertised, so the prefix keeps its 301
	# to the portal root, query preserved. /surveys and /collections used to do the same because
	# there was nothing behind them; the engine now emits an index page at each, the box serves it
	# at that exact path, so those two prefixes PASS THROUGH to the reader like any other portal
	# path. Redirecting them here would make two canonical, indexable hub pages unreachable from
	# the public name, and the doctor's tier-3 leg probes both for exactly that reason.
	@stations_bare path /stations /stations/
	handle @stations_bare {
		redir https://{$AUSMT_PUBLIC_NAME}/{ausmt_qs} permanent
	}
	# ---- end path-url contract ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	# ---- TIME-SERIES HAND-OFF ROUTES --------------------------------------------------------------
	# /go/ts/<survey>/<station>/<level> answers 302 to the ONE NCI THREDDS fileServer URL for that exact
	# file. AusMT hands the reader off; it never proxies, re-hosts or re-zips a byte.
	#
	# THE TABLE IS THE SUPPRESSION. has_time_series is projected for WITHHELD stations too (existence
	# semantics: an embargo must not flip a discovery flag), so mtcat.json hands an enumerator every
	# segment of one of these paths. Suppression therefore cannot live in discovery and must live in
	# RESOLUTION: ts-routes.map is GENERATED from the verified-resource registers by
	# deploy/scripts/gen_ts_routes.py and carries ONLY open-access, `review: verified`, non-level2 rows,
	# so an unlisted path cannot produce a Location at all - `default ""` 404s it by construction, not
	# by filter. The table is committed, so that membership is greppable in review and testable in CI
	# (deploy/tests/test_frontdoor_ts_routes.py drives these directives against a real Caddy).
	#
	# KEYED ON {ausmt_pathurl_rest} (declared at :202-205), the raw URI up to the first '?'. That is
	# the OPPOSITE of the preserve-the-query rule at :190-194, by design: a query must not ride
	# onto an NCI URL, so it is stripped BEFORE the lookup and can never reach the Location - which is
	# also what stops `?u=https://evil.example` turning this into an open redirect. Chained maps resolve
	# LAZILY (the point of the note at :187), so this map may read the other map's output.
	#
	# TERMINAL AT THE FRONT DOOR: neither handle proxies, so no /go/ts/ request ever reaches the box and
	# the C47 wall-2 allowlist (deploy/docker/caddy/Caddyfile) is untouched by construction. The map
	# file is mounted read-only by compose.yaml and validated with the config by install-frontdoor.sh
	# (`caddy validate` FAILS on a missing import, which is the point: the table is part of the config).
	# To withdraw every hand-off route: empty ts-routes.map and re-run the installer (RUNBOOK 10.1b).
	map {ausmt_pathurl_rest} {ausmt_ts_target} {
		import /etc/caddy/ts-routes.map
		default ""
	}
	@ts_known expression {ausmt_ts_target} != ""
	handle @ts_known {
		redir https://thredds.nci.org.au/thredds/fileServer/{ausmt_ts_target} 302
	}
	@ts_prefix path /go/ts /go/ts/*
	handle @ts_prefix {
		respond "not found" 404
	}
	# ---- end time-series hand-off routes -------------------------------------------------------------

	# Deny-by-default: every other /gateway path (both slash forms) AND any non-GET /add-survey.html is
	# an explicit 404, never proxied. Listed AFTER the allow handles so the public subset wins; a
	# curator/admin path, a bare /gateway, or a wrong-method public route lands here.
	@nonpublic path /gateway /gateway/* /add-survey.html /add-survey.html/
	handle @nonpublic {
		respond "not found" 404
	}

	# ---- The public reader: everything else (portal static index/about + /data) proxies to the box
	# public-subset listener over the tailnet. {$AUSMT_BOX_READER_UPSTREAM} is e.g. http://ausmt-box:8445
	# (the box's MagicDNS name + the tailscale-serve reader port) — config-side name, supplied from .env.
	handle {
		import box_upstream
	}
}

# >>> legacy-redirect
# ---- Legacy-name permanent redirect ---------------------------------------------------
# {$AUSMT_LEGACY_REDIRECT_NAME} (the retired public name, e.g. ausmt.au) answers every request with
# a 301 to the SAME path and query on {$AUSMT_PUBLIC_NAME} (the canonical AuScope name). The block
# is REDIRECT-ONLY by design: no reverse_proxy (a request must never reach the reader wall under
# the legacy identity), no log (the masked access log stays on the canonical block ONLY, so a
# redirect hop is never counted as a visit and a legacy hit cannot double-count), and no headers
# (deliberately no HSTS here: the block stays minimal and the canonical site sets HSTS once the
# client lands there). The bare hostname address turns on automatic HTTPS for this name too, so the
# edge holds certificates for BOTH names and the https:// leg itself 301s.
#
# THIS BLOCK IS TEMPLATED, NOT ALWAYS-ON. Caddyfile `{$VAR}` interpolation cannot conditionally
# omit a site block: with AUSMT_LEGACY_REDIRECT_NAME unset the address would render EMPTY, which is
# a parse error, i.e. Caddy would fail to start on exactly the deploy that has no legacy name.
# install-frontdoor.sh therefore renders Caddyfile.rendered from this file, stripping everything
# from the `# >>> legacy-redirect` marker to the `# <<< legacy-redirect` marker when the var is
# unset, and compose mounts the RENDERED file. Keep the whole block (and these comments) inside the
# two markers, and never edit the markers themselves.
{$AUSMT_LEGACY_REDIRECT_NAME} {
	# Permanent (301) by contract: crawlers must learn the move is final, so `permanent` may never
	# soften. {uri} carries the full request URI, path AND query, so a deep link like
	# /data/mtcat.schema.json?v=1.2 (the schema $id served before the canonical-name migration)
	# keeps resolving through the redirect.
	redir https://{$AUSMT_PUBLIC_NAME}{uri} permanent
}
# <<< legacy-redirect
